{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1200781-hearing-distance-of-a-normal-howl","id":1200781,"title":"Hearing distance of a normal howl?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-03-29T06:26:40-07:00","id":1200781,"title":"Hearing distance of a normal howl?","content":"Hey,<br /> <br /> I've been wondering if there's a distance defined for a normal person with normal hearing capabilities of a normal, unmodified (by gift or in any other way) howl.<br /> <br /> Howl of Death works by that mechanism, so I assume it is written somewhere, but I cannot, for the life of me, find it.<br /> <br /> Thank you in advance!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23978-tatacavalier","name":"TataCavalier","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"18"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-29T06:46:43-07:00","id":1200786,"title":"","content":"Use your best judgement. Wolves can hear each other up to between 6 and 10 miles depending on terrain. I would normally say that if they are in the same scene as you, you can pretty much hit them with it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1619-derzhuzad","name":"Derzhuzad","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1619_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-31T11:09:15-07:00","id":1201671,"title":"","content":"It would depend on a lot of factors because various things can muffle, cover, or amplify sound. The farther sound travels, the less decibels it has. Eventually it hits the point where human ears cannot hear it, but some animal ears still can.<br /> <br /> Wolf howls are only 90-115 decibels. That is at the level of lawn mowers, chainsaws, and car horns. Just above a screaming baby at its low scale, and below the center of a live rock concert at its high scale.. So in a city, that howl might only be heard for a few city blocks as the buildings and their materials block and muffle the sound as the other city noises also covers it up.<br /> <br /> But a maximum between 2-6 miles depending on terrain should be a decent guideline. That six miles limit would have to be in very desolate areas from mountain or hill tops so it could go throughout a valley with good atmospheric conditions without competing sounds.<br /> <br /> Of course, at the extreme distance, a wolf howl really doesn't sound like a howl anymore. It's too muted. Howl of Death states that the Red Talon can select one target which it can see clearly, and the person can hear you. That implies the Red Talon must specifically target a person relatively close with its howl and that its howl is still relatively loud and not dissipated from travelling a long while. For all practical purposes, I would say that the limit of Howl of Death has to be in usual tactical combat distances - well within 100 yards/meters,","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-01T01:18:13-07:00","id":1201875,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1200781-hearing-distance-of-a-normal-howl?p=1201671#post1201671\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It would depend on a lot of factors because various things can muffle, cover, or amplify sound. The farther sound travels, the less decibels it has. Eventually it hits the point where human ears cannot hear it, but some animal ears still can.<br /> <br /> Wolf howls are only 90-115 decibels. That is at the level of lawn mowers, chainsaws, and car horns. Just above a screaming baby at its low scale, and below the center of a live rock concert at its high scale.. So in a city, that howl might only be heard for a few city blocks as the buildings and their materials block and muffle the sound as the other city noises also covers it up.<br /> <br /> But a maximum between 2-6 miles depending on terrain should be a decent guideline. That six miles limit would have to be in very desolate areas from mountain or hill tops so it could go throughout a valley with good atmospheric conditions without competing sounds.<br /> <br /> Of course, at the extreme distance, a wolf howl really doesn't sound like a howl anymore. It's too muted. Howl of Death states that the Red Talon can select one target which it can see clearly, and the person can hear you. That implies the Red Talon must specifically target a person relatively close with its howl and that its howl is still relatively loud and not dissipated from travelling a long while. For all practical purposes, I would say that the limit of Howl of Death has to be in usual tactical combat distances - well within 100 yards/meters,</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, I am fully aware it states the Red Talon must -clearly- see the target. However, I'm asking specifically on the howl hearing distance (for normal humans, leave modifiers aside such as Auspex and whatnot).<br /> <br /> The reason I am focused on the howl distance is because this can be used as a combo with another Gift (Territory) which allows the Garou's senses to be transmitted to a marked location.<br /> Now, when that happens, the physical body of the wolf is away, but their senses (their sight, for example) is right where the marking is. Which an enemy could just may be.<br /> <br /> That covers up the requirement of sight, which leaves us with hearing. But yes, thank you. You have answered my question to the fullest. I suppose I'll run this by my ST and see what they think.<br /> <br /> Thank you both!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23978-tatacavalier","name":"TataCavalier","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"18"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}